{{also|checkout}}
==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|checks out|checking out|checked out}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To confirm and pay for goods and services at a [[facility]] (e.g.: [[supermarket]], online store, [[hotel]]) when leaving.
#: ''Be sure to '''check out''' of the hotel before noon.''
#: ''I'm done shopping, so I'll go '''check out''' now.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[withdraw]] (an [[item]]), as from a [[library]], and have the withdrawal recorded.
#: ''He '''checked''' his favorite mystery '''out''' for the twenty-third time.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To record (someone) as leaving the premises or as taking something therefrom, as from a library or shop.
#: ''The desk clerk '''checked out''' the family that had been staying in room 322.''
#: ''The library assistant was '''checking''' people '''out'''.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[examine]], [[inspect]], look at closely, [[ogle]]; to [[investigate]].
#: ''He was hanging out at the beach, '''checking out''' the young women in bikinis.''
#: ''He '''checked out''' the rumor, and managed to verify that it was true.''
#: '''''Check''' it '''out'''! Best prices in town.''
#: '''''Check''' ''this'' '''out'''! They just arrested the Mayor!''
#: ''When you're there, '''check out''' the Cheddar Cheese Museum! It's a hoot!''
#: '''''Check''' us '''out''' on the Web at [https://en.wiktionary.org en.wiktionary.org]!''
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To obtain [[source code]] from a [[repository]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[disinterested]] in an activity and cease to participate in more than a perfunctory manner; to become [[uncooperative]].
#* {{quote-text|year=2010|author=S. Greggory Johnson III|title=The Black Professoriat: Negotiating a Habitable Space in the Academy|page=189|passage=The purpose of this exercise was to ignite reactions from students, but over the few years I used it, it backfired, culminating in a situation where I lost a significant number of the white students, who just "'''checked out'''" for the rest of the semester.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[catatonic]] or otherwise [[nonresponsive]].
#* '''2007''' Susan Nathiel, ''Daughters of Madness: Growing Up and Older With a Mentally Ill Mother'', p. 42:
#*: Even during those years, there would be a lot of times she just '''checked out'''. She would be sitting there looking at her nails and she'd just be gone.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[leave]] in a [[hurry]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|euphemistic|by extension}} To [[die]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To prove (after an investigation) to be the case / [[in order]].
#: ''The first two leads '''check out'''; I'll assume the third one is also valid.''
#: ''Their stories '''checked out'''.''

